1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
Why would thousands of tiny fish make a wave
when a bird attacks?

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
In Mexico’s sulfur springs, sulfur mollies
crowd near the oxygen-rich surface.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,500
When a threat arrives, fish dive
and flick their tails.

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,000
Neighbors copy the movement, so a ripple
of dives spreads across the shoal.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The fish are not travelling across the pond
as one giant body—the wave is a pattern
passed from fish to fish.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Experiments found that these displays made birds wait
about twice as long before attacking again,
and reduced capture success for one predator.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
But the birds are adapting too.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
A 2026 field study found kingfishers often attacked
near the shoal’s edges, where fewer waves were triggered.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The fish, in turn, produced stronger waves
when danger returned nearby.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
It is a moving arms race—built without a leader.
